{"abstract":"Bahr and Hutton recently developed a new approach to calculating correct compilers directly from specifications of their correctness. However, the methodology only considers converging behaviour of the source language, which means that the compiler could potentially produce arbitrary, erroneous code for source programs that diverge. In this article, we show how the methodology can naturally be extended to support the calculation of compilers that address both convergent and divergent behaviour simultaneously, without the need for separate reasoning for each aspect. Our approach is based on the use of the partiality monad to make divergence explicit, together with the use of strong bisimilarity to support equational-style calculations, but also generalises to other forms of effect by changing the underlying monad.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Bahr, Patrick","Hutton, Graham"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381992"],"event":{"acronym":"ICFP","title":"ACM SIGPLAN International Conference on Functional Programming"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Nottingham"],"keyword":["Agda","Bisimulation","Virtual machine","monad","program calculation","verified compiler"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"monadic compiler calculation functional pearl","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3547624","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381992","identifier":[{"id":"85139595909","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-31885823-95cc-4b43-af63-52694ba86668"],"keyword":["Agda","bisimulation","monad","program calculation","verified compiler","virtual machine"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-7EF587B23700D713B1FB151673574DB1"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/90391817/mococa_paper.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3547624","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Programming Logic and Semantics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"university of nottingham","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Nottingham","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Patrick","key":"bahrpau","last":"Bahr","locid":"5e271e6a-7a91-47bd-9f12-3b73f4e0d7ac","name":"Bahr, Patrick","orcid":"0000-0003-1600-8261","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Graham","key":"huttonpau","last":"Hutton","name":"Hutton, Graham","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3547624","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the ACM on Programming Languages","volume":"6"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85139595909"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-31885823-95cc-4b43-af63-52694ba86668"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Monadic Compiler Calculation (Functional Pearl)","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381992","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Functional reactive programming (FRP) provides a high-level interface for implementing reactive systems in a declarative manner. However, this high-level interface has to be carefully reined in to ensure that programs can in fact be executed in practice. Specifically, one must ensure that FRP programs are productive, causal, and can be implemented without introducing space leaks. In recent years, modal types have been demonstrated to be an effective tool to ensure these operational properties. In this paper, we present Rattus, a modal FRP language that extends and simplifies previous modal FRP calculi while still maintaining the operational guarantees for productivity, causality, and space leaks. The simplified type system makes Rattus a practical programming language that can be integrated with existing functional programming languages. To demonstrate this, we have implemented a shallow embedding of Rattus in Haskell that allows the programmer to write Rattus code in familiar Haskell syntax and seamlessly integrate it with regular Haskell code. Thus Rattus combines the benefits enjoyed by FRP libraries such as Yampa, namely access to a rich library ecosystem (e.g. for graphics programming), with the strong operational guarantees offered by a bespoke type system. To establish the productivity, causality, and memory properties of the language, we prove type soundness using a logical relations argument fully mechanised in the Coq proof assistant.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Bahr, Patrick"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383229"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Functional Reactive Programming","Garbage collection","Haskell","Modal Types","Type systems"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"modal frp for all functional reactive programming without space leaks in haskell","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1017/S0956796822000132","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383229","identifier":[{"id":"85145772874","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-8551f92a-41df-407a-8fa6-9c1f0a28d7fd"],"keyword":["Haskell","functional reactive programming","garbage collection","modal types","type systems"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-B60819BCF23FD7338C0249D1C8DE26BB"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1017/S0956796822000132","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/92114519/rattus_paper.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Programming Logic and Semantics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Patrick","key":"bahrpau","last":"Bahr","locid":"5e271e6a-7a91-47bd-9f12-3b73f4e0d7ac","name":"Bahr, Patrick","orcid":"0000-0003-1600-8261","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1017/s0956796822000132","issn":["09567968","14697653"],"issn_org":["09567968"],"journal":"Journal of Functional Programming","paperid":"e15","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","status":"p","title":"Journal of Functional Programming","volume":"32"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85145772874"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-8551f92a-41df-407a-8fa6-9c1f0a28d7fd"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Modal FRP for all: Functional reactive programming without space leaks in Haskell","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383229","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"When designing languages for functional reactive programming (FRP) the main challenge is to provide the user with a simple, flexible interface for writing programs on a high level of abstraction while ensuring that all programs can be implemented efficiently in a low-level language. To meet this challenge, a new family of modal FRP languages has been proposed, in which variants of Nakano's guarded fixed point operator are used for writing recursive programs guaranteeing properties such as causality and productivity. As an apparent extension to this it has also been suggested to use Linear Temporal Logic (LTL) as a language for reactive programming through the Curry-Howard isomorphism, allowing properties such as termination, liveness and fairness to be encoded in types. However, these two ideas are in conflict with each other, since the fixed point operator introduces non-termination into the inductive types that are supposed to provide termination guarantees. In this paper we show that by regarding the modal time step operator of LTL a submodality of the one used for guarded recursion (rather than equating them), one can obtain a modal type system capable of expressing liveness properties while retaining the power of the guarded fixed point operator. We introduce the language Lively RaTT, a modal FRP language with a guarded fixed point operator and an `until' type constructor as in LTL, and show how to program with events and fair streams. Using a step-indexed Kripke logical relation we prove operational properties of Lively RaTT including productivity and causality as well as the termination and liveness properties expected of types from LTL. Finally, we prove that the type system of Lively RaTT guarantees the absence of implicit space leaks.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Bahr, Patrick","Graulund, Christian Uldal","Møgelberg, Rasmus Ejlers"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384887"],"event":{"acronym":"POPL","title":"ACM SIGPLAN Symposium on Principles of Programming Languages"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Curry Howard Isomorphism","Functional Reactive Programming","Guarded Recursion","Linear Temporal Logic","Modal Type Systems"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"diamonds are not forever liveness in reactive programming with guarded recursion","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3434283","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384887","identifier":[{"id":"85099045012","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-f57dac1e-2437-41b9-b7a7-2f50ead52087"],"keyword":["Curry-Howard Isomorphism","Functional Reactive Programming","Guarded Recursion","Linear Temporal Logic","Modal Type Systems"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-2FBFB4DE073536D177B54DF32565A566"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85724929/diamondsnotforever.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3434283","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Programming Logic and Semantics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Patrick","key":"bahrpau","last":"Bahr","locid":"5e271e6a-7a91-47bd-9f12-3b73f4e0d7ac","name":"Bahr, Patrick","orcid":"0000-0003-1600-8261","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christian Uldal","key":"graulundpau","last":"Graulund","name":"Graulund, Christian Uldal","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rasmus Ejlers","key":"møgelbergpau","last":"Møgelberg","locid":"10003d22-a201-4b64-88e3-b423bc76fcc2","name":"Møgelberg, Rasmus Ejlers","orcid":"0000-0003-0386-4376","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3434283","edition":"POPL","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","series":"Proceedings of the ACM on Programming Languages","status":"p","title":"ACM SIGPLAN Symposium on Principles of Programming Languages","volume":"5"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85099045012"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-f57dac1e-2437-41b9-b7a7-2f50ead52087"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Diamonds are not forever: Liveness in reactive programming with guarded recursion","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384887","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"In 'Calculating Correct Compilers' we developed a new approach to calculating compilers directly from specifications of their correctness. Our approach only required elementary reasoning techniques, and has been used to calculate compilers for a wide range of language features and their combination. However, the methodology was focused on stack-based target machines, whereas real compilers often target register-based machines. In this article, we show how our approach can naturally be adapted to calculate compilers for register machines.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Bahr, Patrick","Hutton, Graham"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383900"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Nottingham"],"keyword":["Coq","preorder","program calculation","register machines","verified compiler"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"calculating correct compilers ii return of the register machines","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85295380/bahr20jfp_paper.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383900","identifier":[{"id":"85094911389","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-b4eb29bf-4332-4676-a7c8-2899ab01b5b4"],"keyword":["Coq","preorder","program calculation","register machines","verified compiler"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-D0412765E62FD2E1E988C6FAB680754F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85295380/bahr20jfp_paper.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Programming Logic and Semantics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"university of nottingham","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Nottingham","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Patrick","key":"bahrpau","last":"Bahr","locid":"5e271e6a-7a91-47bd-9f12-3b73f4e0d7ac","name":"Bahr, Patrick","orcid":"0000-0003-1600-8261","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Graham","key":"huttonpau","last":"Hutton","name":"Hutton, Graham","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1017/s0956796820000209","issn":["09567968","14697653"],"issn_org":["09567968"],"journal":"Journal of Functional Programming","paperid":"e25","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","status":"p","title":"Journal of Functional Programming","volume":"30"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85094911389"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-b4eb29bf-4332-4676-a7c8-2899ab01b5b4"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Calculating Correct Compilers II: Return of the Register Machines","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383900","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Background: Long QT syndrome (LQTS) is a rare genetic disorder and a major preventable cause of sudden cardiac death in the young. A causal rare genetic variant with large effect size is identified in up to 80% of probands (genotype positive) and cascade family screening shows incomplete penetrance of genetic variants. Furthermore, a proportion of cases meeting diagnostic criteria for LQTS remain genetically elusive despite genetic testing of established genes (genotype negative). These observations raise the possibility that common genetic variants with small effect size contribute to the clinical picture of LQTS. This study aimed to characterize and quantify the contribution of common genetic variation to LQTS disease susceptibility. Methods: We conducted genome-wide association studies (GWAS) followed by transethnic meta-analysis in 1,656 unrelated LQTS patients of European or Japanese ancestry and 9,890 controls to identify susceptibility single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs). We estimated the SNP heritability (h2SNP) of LQTS and tested the genetic correlation between LQTS susceptibility and other cardiac traits. Furthermore, we tested the aggregate effect of the 68 SNPs previously associated with QTc in the general population using a polygenic risk score (PRSQT). Results: Genome-wide association analysis identified three loci associated with LQTS at genome-wide statistical significance (P<5x10-8) near NOS1AP, KCNQ1 and KLF12, and one missense variant in KCNE1 (p.Asp85Asn) at the suggestive threshold (P<10-6). Heritability analyses showed that ~15% of variance in overall LQTS susceptibility was attributable to common genetic variation (h2SNP 0.148; standard error [SE] 0.019). LQTS susceptibility showed a strong genome-wide genetic correlation with the QT interval in the general population (rg=0.40, P=3.2x10-3). PRSQT was greater in LQTS cases compared to controls (P<10-13), and notably, among LQTS patients PRSQT was greater in genotype negative compared to genotype positive patients (P<0.005). Conclusions: This work establishes an important role for common genetic variation in susceptibility to LQTS. We demonstrate overlap between genetic control of the QT interval in the general population and genetic factors contributing to LQTS susceptibility. Using polygenic risk score analyses aggregating common genetic variants that modulate the QT interval in the general population, we provide evidence for a polygenic architecture in genotype negative LQTS.","author_count":106,"contributors":["Lahrouchi, Najim","Tadros, Rafik","Crotti, Lia","Mizusawa, Yuka","Postema, Pieter G","Beekman, Leander","Walsh, Roddy","Hasegawa, Kanae","Barc, Julien","Ernsting, Marko","Turkowski, Kari L","Mazzanti, Andrea","Beckmann, Britt M","Shimamoto, Keiko","Diamant, Ulla-Britt","Wijeyeratne, Yanushi D","Kucho, Yu","Robyns, Tomas","Ishikawa, Taisuke","Arbelo, Elena","Christiansen, Michael","Winbo, Annika","Jabbari, Reza","Lubitz, Steven A","Steinfurt, Johannes","Rudic, Boris","Loeys, Bart","Shoemaker, M Ben","Weeke, Peter E","Pfeiffer, Ryan","Davies, Brianna","Andorin, Antoine","Hofman, Nynke","Dagradi, Federica","Pedrazzini, Matteo","Tester, David J","Bos, J Martijn","Sarquella-Brugada, Georgia","Campuzano, Óscar","Platonov, Pyotr G","Stallmeyer, Birgit","Zumhagen, Sven","Nannenberg, Eline A","Veldink, Jan H","van den Berg, Leonard H","Al-Chalabi, Ammar","Shaw, Christopher E","Shaw, Pamela J","Morrison, Karen E","Andersen, Peter M","Müller-Nurasyid, Martina","Cusi, Daniele","Barlassina, Cristina","Galan, Pilar","Lathrop, Mark","Munter, Markus","Werge, Thomas","Ribasés, Marta","Aung, Tin","Khor, Chiea C","Ozaki, Mineo","Lichtner, Peter","Meitinger, Thomas","van Tintelen, J Peter","Hoedemaekers, Yvonne","Denjoy, Isabelle","Leenhardt, Antoine","Napolitano, Carlo","Shimizu, Wataru","Schott, Jean-Jacques","Gourraud, Jean-Baptiste","Makiyama, Takeru","Ohno, Seiko","Itoh, Hideki","Krahn, Andrew D","Antzelevitch, Charles","Roden, Dan M","Saenen, 