{"abstract":"Functional reactive programming (FRP) is a paradigm for programming with signals and events, allowing the user to describe reactive programs on a high level of abstraction. For this to make sense, an FRP language must ensure that all programs are causal, and can be implemented without introducing space leaks and time leaks. To this end, some FRP languages do not give direct access to signals, but just to signal functions. Recently, modal types have been suggested as an alternative approach to ensuring causality in FRP languages in the synchronous case, giving direct access to the signal and event abstractions. This paper presents Simply RaTT, a new modal calculus for reactive programming. Unlike prior calculi, Simply RaTT uses a Fitch-style approach to modal types, which simplifies the type system and makes programs more concise. Echoing a previous result by Krishnaswami for a different language, we devise an operational semantics that safely executes Simply RaTT programs without space leaks. We also identify a source of time leaks present in other modal FRP languages: The unfolding of fixed points in delayed computations. The Fitch-style presentation allows an easy way to rules out these leaks, which appears not to be possible in the more traditional dual context approach.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Bahr, Patrick","Graulund, Christian Uldal","Møgelberg, Rasmus Ejlers"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382853"],"event":{"acronym":"ICFP","title":"ACM SIGPLAN International Conference on Functional Programming"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Causality","Fitch Style Calculus","Functional Reactive Programming","Modal Types","Operational semantics","Reactive Programming","Signal Functions","Space Leaks","Time Leaks","Type systems"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"simply ratt a fitch style modal calculus for reactive programming","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3341713","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382853","identifier":[{"id":"85085938198","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-69cb3ec2-c472-47e3-8eb9-08b5a6ed43ed"],"keyword":["Causality","Fitch-Style Calculus","Functional Reactive Programming","Modal Types","Operational Semantics","Reactive Programming","Signal Functions","Space Leaks","Time Leaks","Type Systems"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-90437D4336B3A7B39B54D1F97FDA8933"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84375815/icfp2019.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3341713","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Programming Logic and Semantics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Patrick","key":"bahrpau","last":"Bahr","locid":"5e271e6a-7a91-47bd-9f12-3b73f4e0d7ac","name":"Bahr, Patrick","orcid":"0000-0003-1600-8261","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christian Uldal","key":"graulundpau","last":"Graulund","name":"Graulund, Christian Uldal","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rasmus Ejlers","key":"møgelbergpau","last":"Møgelberg","locid":"10003d22-a201-4b64-88e3-b423bc76fcc2","name":"Møgelberg, Rasmus Ejlers","orcid":"0000-0003-0386-4376","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3341713","edition":"IFCP","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the ACM on Programming Languages","volume":"3"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85085938198"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-69cb3ec2-c472-47e3-8eb9-08b5a6ed43ed"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Simply RaTT: A Fitch-style Modal Calculus for Reactive Programming","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382853","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"The infinitary lambda calculi pioneered by Kennaway et al. extend the basic lambda calculus by metric completion to infinite terms and reductions. Depending on the chosen metric, the res- ulting infinitary calculi exhibit different notions of strictness. To obtain infinitary normalisation and infinitary confluence properties for these calculi, Kennaway et al. extend β-reduction with infinitely many ‘⊥-rules’, which contract meaningless terms directly to ⊥. Three of the resulting Böhm reduction calculi have unique infinitary normal forms corresponding to Böhm-like trees. In this paper we develop a corresponding theory of infinitary lambda calculi based on ideal completion instead of metric completion. We show that each of our calculi conservatively ex- tends the corresponding metric-based calculus. Three of our calculi are infinitarily normalising and confluent; their unique infinitary normal forms are exactly the Böhm-like trees of the cor- responding metric-based calculi. Our calculi dispense with the infinitely many ⊥-rules of the metric-based calculi. The fully non-strict calculus (called 111) consists of only β-reduction, while the other two calculi (called 001 and 101) require two additional rules that precisely state their strictness properties: λx.⊥ → ⊥ (for 001) and ⊥ M → ⊥ (for 001 and 101).","author_count":1,"contributors":["Bahr, Patrick"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384216"],"event":{"acronym":"FSCD","title":"International Conference on Formal Structures for Computation and Deduction"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Böhm trees","Confluence","Lambda calculus","infinitary rewriting","meaningless terms"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"strict ideal completions of the lambda calculus","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.FSCD.2018.8","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384216","identifier":[{"id":"85049793691","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-c96e7a12-6a40-41e9-b92c-6a0093acea45"],"keyword":["Böhm trees","confluence","infinitary rewriting","lambda calculus","meaningless terms"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-4FCD226B687572B66051C28D0AAB3710"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.FSCD.2018.8","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83233524/LIPIcs_FSCD_2018_8.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Programming Logic and Semantics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Patrick","key":"bahrpau","last":"Bahr","locid":"5e271e6a-7a91-47bd-9f12-3b73f4e0d7ac","name":"Bahr, Patrick","orcid":"0000-0003-1600-8261","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.4230/lipics.fscd.2018.8","publisher":"Schloss Dagstuhl - Leibniz-Zentrum fuer Informatik GmbH","status":"p","title":"3rd International Conference on Formal Structures for Computation and Deduction (FSCD 2018)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85049793691"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-c96e7a12-6a40-41e9-b92c-6a0093acea45"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Strict Ideal Completions of the Lambda Calculus","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384216","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Term graph rewriting provides a formalism for implementing term rewriting in an efficient manner by emulating duplication via sharing. Infinitary term rewriting has been introduced to study infinite term reduction sequences. Such infinite reductions can be used to model non-strict evaluation. In this paper, we unify term graph rewriting and infinitary term rewriting thereby addressing both components of lazy evaluation: non-strictness and sharing. In contrast to previous attempts to formalise infinitary term graph rewriting, our approach is based on a simple and natural generalisation of the modes of convergence of infinitary term rewriting. We show that this new approach is better suited for infinitary term graph rewriting as it is simpler and more general. The latter is demonstrated by the fact that our notions of convergence give rise to two independent canonical and exhaustive constructions of infinite term graphs from finite term graphs via metric and ideal completion. In addition, we show that our notions of convergence on term graphs are sound w.r.t. the ones employed in infinitary term rewriting in the sense that convergence is preserved by unravelling term graphs to terms. Moreover, the resulting infinitary term graph calculi provide a unified framework for both infinitary term rewriting and term graph rewriting, which makes it possible to study the correspondences between these two worlds more closely.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Bahr, Patrick"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384603"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Convergence","Graph Rewriting","ideal completion","infinitary rewriting","metric completion"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"convergence in infinitary term graph rewriting systems is simple","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83263576/paper.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384603","identifier":[{"id":"85052563306","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-e23f5194-f63a-417d-95f1-6995d1e48973"],"keyword":["convergence","graph rewriting","ideal completion","infinitary rewriting","metric completion"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-E72491D393A04797A72192F30C75B17E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83263576/paper.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Programming Logic and Semantics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Patrick","key":"bahrpau","last":"Bahr","locid":"5e271e6a-7a91-47bd-9f12-3b73f4e0d7ac","name":"Bahr, Patrick","orcid":"0000-0003-1600-8261","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1017/s0960129518000166","issn":["09601295","14698072"],"issn_org":["09601295"],"journal":"Mathematical Structures in Computer Science","pages":"1-52","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","status":"p","title":"Mathematical Structures in Computer Science"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85052563306"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-e23f5194-f63a-417d-95f1-6995d1e48973"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Convergence in infinitary term graph rewriting systems is simple","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384603","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"author_count":13,"contributors":["Vermesan, Ovidiu","Eisenhauer, Markus","Serrano, Martin","GUILLEMIN, Patrick","Sundmaeker, Harald","Tragos, E.Z.","Valiño, J.","Copigneaux, B.","Presser, Mirko","Aagaard, Annabeth","Bahr, R."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-158172"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the next generation internet of things hyperconnectivity and embedded intelligence at the edge","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.au.dk/ws/files/137054808/The_Next_Generation_Internet_of_Things.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-158172","identifier":[{"id":"85074709544","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-3269870d-0107-431d-b870-87f43df7119c"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-A2999C8D07EB44FBDADDC3D7907BCFD1"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.au.dk/ws/files/137054808/The_Next_Generation_Internet_of_Things.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.riverpublishers.com/pdf/ebook/RP_E9788770220071.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Aarhus BSS, Department of Business Development and Technology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Ovidiu","key":"vermesanpau","last":"Vermesan","name":"Vermesan, Ovidiu","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Mar